Keep me awake License

Keep me awake is mine: Akshay Bajpai (https://akshaybajpai.com) made it, and
the code, the design, the name and the little sun all belong to me. The
source is out in the open so you can see exactly what it does. That makes it
readable, not open source.

What you're welcome to do

- Use the builds I publish, free, on your own machines, for yourself or your
  own work.
- Read the code, build it yourself, and tinker with your own copy.
- Send me a fix or an idea. If you do, you're saying it's yours to give and
  I'm free to use it however I like.

What needs a yes from me first

- Copying, re-uploading, selling or bundling Keep me awake, changed or not.
- Publishing your own version of it, or something built from it.
- Using the name or the sun-on-the-horizon artwork for anything else.

Ask through https://akshaybajpai.com. I'm reasonable.

Wherever the code is hosted (GitHub, say), that site's own terms may let its
users view and fork the repository there. This license adds nothing to those
terms beyond what is written above.

The part where I'm careful

Keep me awake asks your operating system not to sleep. That's all it does,
but it means your machine stays on: the battery drains, things get warm, the
screen may stay lit, and a laptop left awake is a laptop left unlocked for
longer. Whether that's fine, at home or on a work machine with its own rules,
is your call, not mine.

So: you use Keep me awake at your own risk. It comes as is, with no promise
that it works, keeps working, suits your purpose, or won't break something.
I'm not responsible for anything that happens because you used it, or
couldn't: lost work, drained batteries, missed meetings, damaged hardware,
data loss, policy trouble, or anything else, however it comes about.

